      "text": "We start with the given equations of the lines:\n1. \\( y = 2x + 13 \\)\n2. \\( 6y + ax = 6 \\)\n\nTo determine if the lines are perpendicular, we need to check the slopes of both lines. The general form of a linear equation is \\( y = mx + b \\), where \\( m \\) is the slope of the line.\n\n### Step 1: Identify the slope of the first line\nThe first line, \\( y = 2x + 13 \\), is already in slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\). Therefore, the slope of the first line is:\n\\[\nm_1 = 2\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Find the slope of the second line\nRewrite the second line equation \\( 6y + ax = 6 \\) in slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\). Start by isolating \\( y \\):\n\\[\n6y = -ax + 6\n\\]\nDivide every term by 6:\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n\\]\nFrom this form, we can see that the slope of the second line is:\n\\[\nm_2 = -\\frac{a}{6}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Determine the condition for perpendicular lines\nTwo lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is \\(-1\\). Therefore, we set up the equation:\n\\[\nm_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1\n\\]\nSubstitute the slopes \\( m_1 = 2 \\) and \\( m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6} \\):\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -1\n\\]\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n-\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac{a}{3} = -1\n\\]\nMultiply both sides by \\(-3\\) to solve for \\( a \\):\n\\[\na = 3\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThe value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
